---
/**
 * Unix Timestamp Converter — 미국 영어판 여섯 번째 도구(유닉스 타임스탬프 변환). 한국어판 /tools/unix-time/ 와
 * 계산(lib/unix-time.ts)·스크립트(scripts/unix-time.ts 의 EN 사전)를 같이 쓰고, 문장은 영어로 새로 썼다(번역 아님).
 * 조사표 미국 칸 '같음'(POSIX 4.19).
 *
 * 소제목은 미국 구글 자동완성에서 왔다(keywords/us-unix-time-2026-10-20.csv):
 *   unix time / epoch time now·unix timestamp now / unix timestamp milliseconds·format / timestamp to date excel /
 *   timestamp to date python·js·sql·postgresql·sql server·pandas / unix timestamp discord·generator /
 *   unix timestamp to utc·to est
 * 시간대는 UTC 와 '이 기기 시간대'만 — 미국 표준시 오프셋·서머타임 규칙(15 U.S.C. 260a, 49 CFR 71)은 원문을 확인하지
 * 않아 EST·PST 숫자를 쓰지 않는다. 본문 예시 숫자는 전부 아래 frontmatter 에서 lib 함수로 계산한다.
 */
import BaseHead from "../../../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../../../components/AdSlot.astro";
import ClarityHead from "../../../../components/ClarityHead.astro";
import GtagHead from "../../../../components/GtagHead.astro";
import TrackLinks from "../../../../components/TrackLinks.astro";
import AdSenseHead from "../../../../components/AdSenseHead.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../../../styles/global.css";
import { INT32_MAX, toParts, enText, isoText, secondsText, dateValue, timeValue } from "../../../../lib/unix-time";

const title = "Unix Timestamp Converter — Epoch Time to Date (Seconds or Milliseconds)";
const description =
  "Convert a Unix timestamp in seconds or 13-digit milliseconds to a date in UTC and your time zone, or a date to epoch time. Plus Excel, Python, SQL and Discord.";

/** 처음 보이는 예 — 2026-10-19 03:34:56 UTC */
const BASE_MS = 1_792_380_896_000;
const p0 = toParts(BASE_MS, 0);
const BASE_S = secondsText(BASE_MS);
const zero = enText(toParts(0, 0));
const minus1 = enText(toParts(-1000, 0));
const y2038 = enText(toParts(INT32_MAX * 1000, 0));
const wrap = enText(toParts(-(INT32_MAX + 1) * 1000, 0));
---

<html lang="en-US">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <ClarityHead />
    <GtagHead />
    <AdSenseHead />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-texture">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <h1>Unix Timestamp Converter (Epoch Time ⇄ Date)</h1>
      <p>
        Found a 10- or 13-digit number in a log, a database row or an API response? Paste it here to see the date and time it
        stands for. It works the other way too: enter a date and time and you get the Unix timestamp. Change either side and the
        other follows.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="zone">Time zone</label>
          <select id="zone" data-seg>
            <option value="utc" selected>UTC</option>
            <option value="device">Your time zone</option>
          </select>
          <p class="hint" id="zone-hint">The date fields and the result are read and written in this time zone. The timestamp itself is the same everywhere.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ts">Unix timestamp</label>
          <input type="text" id="ts" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" value={BASE_S} placeholder="e.g. 1704067200" />
          <p class="hint" id="ts-hint">10 digits are read as seconds, 13 digits as milliseconds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="unit">Unit</label>
          <select id="unit" data-seg>
            <option value="auto" selected>Auto</option>
            <option value="s">Seconds</option>
            <option value="ms">Milliseconds</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="date">Date</label>
          <input type="date" id="date" value={dateValue(p0)} min="1900-01-01" max="9999-12-30" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="time">Time (24-hour, hh:mm:ss)</label>
          <input type="time" id="time" step="1" value={timeValue(p0)} />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="msec">Milliseconds (0–999)</label>
          <input type="number" id="msec" inputmode="numeric" min="0" max="999" step="1" value="0" />
        </div>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-now" type="button" class="secondary">Now</button>
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-dock="hero-value">
          <p class="label" id="hero-label">UTC</p>
          <p class="big" id="hero-value">{enText(p0)}</p>
          <p class="hint" id="hero-sub"></p>
        </div>

        <div class="stat-row" data-receipt id="receipt">
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Seconds (s)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-sec">{BASE_S}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Milliseconds (ms)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-ms">{BASE_MS}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">ISO 8601 (UTC)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-iso">{isoText(BASE_MS, 0)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">ISO 8601 (selected time zone)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-iso-local">{isoText(BASE_MS, 0)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">From now</span>
            <span class="value" id="v-rel">—</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="hint" id="note"></p>
        <p class="meta" id="now-line">Now: —</p>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-download" type="button">Save as image</button>
          <button id="btn-share" type="button" class="secondary">Share</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>What is Unix time (epoch time)?</h2>
      <p>
        Unix time is the <strong>number of seconds since 00:00:00 UTC on January 1, 1970</strong>. That starting point is called
        the epoch, which is why you'll also see it called epoch time or seconds since the epoch. The definition comes from the
        POSIX standard (IEEE Std 1003.1): every day counts as exactly 86,400 seconds, and leap seconds are not added. So 0 is
        {" "}{zero} UTC, and −1,000 milliseconds is {minus1}.
      </p>

      <h2>Unix timestamp now</h2>
      <p>
        The "Now" line under the converter shows the current epoch time in seconds and milliseconds and ticks every second. Press
        <strong>Now</strong> to drop the current timestamp into the converter. Both come from your device's clock, so they are
        only as accurate as that clock.
      </p>

      <h2>Seconds or milliseconds? Count the digits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>10 digits (e.g. {BASE_S})</strong> — a timestamp in seconds. Every moment from September 2001 to November 2286
          has 10 digits.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>13 digits (e.g. {BASE_MS})</strong> — milliseconds. JavaScript's <code>Date.now()</code> and Java's
          {" "}<code>System.currentTimeMillis()</code> return this form. Divide by 1,000 to get seconds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How Auto decides</strong> — a number with a decimal point is read as seconds; a whole number with up to 11
          digits is seconds, and 12 digits or more is milliseconds. Commas and spaces are ignored. Pick a unit if you need a
          different reading.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Unix timestamp to UTC — or to your local time</h2>
      <p>
        A timestamp has no time zone of its own; it marks one instant everywhere. The converter shows that instant in
        <strong>UTC</strong> and, right under it, in your device's time zone. Choose <strong>Your time zone</strong> to read and
        type dates in local time instead (for example, if you're on Eastern time and want "to EST"). Your device applies its own
        daylight saving rules, so a summer date and a winter date can show different UTC offsets.
      </p>

      <h2>Convert a timestamp to a date in Excel or Google Sheets</h2>
      <p>
        Spreadsheets store a date as a serial number where one day equals 1. Divide the seconds by 86,400 and add the epoch date.
        With a timestamp in seconds in cell A1:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>UTC date and time: <code>=A1/86400+DATE(1970,1,1)</code></li>
        <li>13-digit milliseconds: <code>=A1/86400000+DATE(1970,1,1)</code></li>
        <li>Local time: add your UTC offset in hours divided by 24, e.g. <code>=A1/86400+DATE(1970,1,1)+(offset/24)</code>, with a negative offset for time zones behind UTC</li>
        <li>UTC date and time back to a timestamp: <code>=(A1-DATE(1970,1,1))*86400</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If the result shows up as a plain number, format the cell as a date and time, such as <code>yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Timestamp to date in Python, JavaScript and SQL</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Python</strong>: <code>datetime.fromtimestamp({BASE_S}, tz=timezone.utc)</code> (after <code>from datetime import datetime, timezone</code>)</li>
        <li><strong>pandas</strong>: <code>pd.to_datetime({BASE_S}, unit="s")</code> — use <code>unit="ms"</code> for milliseconds</li>
        <li><strong>JavaScript</strong>: <code>new Date({BASE_S} * 1000)</code> — multiply seconds by 1,000 first</li>
        <li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong>: <code>SELECT to_timestamp({BASE_S});</code></li>
        <li><strong>MySQL</strong>: <code>SELECT FROM_UNIXTIME({BASE_S});</code></li>
        <li><strong>SQL Server</strong>: <code>SELECT DATEADD(second, {BASE_S}, '1970-01-01');</code></li>
        <li><strong>Linux / macOS shell</strong>: <code>date +%s</code> prints the current timestamp; GNU <code>date -d @{BASE_S}</code> turns one into a date</li>
      </ul>
      <p>To double-check any of these, paste the number into the converter above and compare.</p>

      <h2>Unix timestamps in Discord messages</h2>
      <p>
        Discord turns <code>&lt;t:{BASE_S}:F&gt;</code> into a date that every reader sees in their own time zone. The letter
        after the second colon sets the style: <code>t</code> and <code>T</code> for short and long time, <code>d</code> and
        <code>D</code> for short and long date, <code>f</code> and <code>F</code> for date with time, and <code>R</code> for
        relative time ("in 3 days"). Use the seconds value from this converter, not milliseconds.
      </p>

      <h2>The Year 2038 problem</h2>
      <p>
        A signed 32-bit integer tops out at 2,147,483,647. As a Unix timestamp that's {y2038} UTC. One second later, systems that
        still keep time in a 32-bit signed integer overflow and wrap around to a negative number — {wrap} UTC. The converter adds a
        note under the result whenever a time is past that limit.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>What about dates before 1970?</summary>
        <p>
          They come out negative: −1 is one second before the epoch, December 31, 1969 23:59:59 UTC. The POSIX formula itself is
          written for 1970 onward, but like most programming languages this converter keeps counting backward at 86,400 seconds a
          day. It handles January 1, 1900 through the year 9999.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Can I convert a timestamp with a decimal point, like 1792380896.789?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. It's read as seconds with up to three decimal places (milliseconds). Microsecond and nanosecond timestamps (16 and
          19 digits) aren't supported.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Why is my result off by a few hours?</summary>
        <p>
          Almost always a time zone mix-up: the number was read as UTC but you expected local time, or the other way around.
          Switch between <strong>UTC</strong> and <strong>Your time zone</strong> and compare. If it's off by a factor of 1,000
          (a date in 1970 or far in the future), the unit is wrong — pick seconds or milliseconds by hand.
        </p>
      </details>

      <p>
        To count the days between two dates, try the <a href="/en-us/tools/date-diff/">date calculator</a>; for a countdown to a
        date, the <a href="/en-us/tools/dday/">days until countdown</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Source: <a href="https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/basedefs/V1_chap04.html" rel="noopener">The Open Group Base Specifications Issue 8 (IEEE Std 1003.1-2024), 4.19 Seconds Since the Epoch</a> (checked October 19, 2026)
      </p>

      <AdSlot label="unix-time-result-en-us" />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
    <style>
      .mono { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
      #hero-value { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    </style>
    <script src="../../../../scripts/unix-time.ts"></script>
    <TrackLinks />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/calc-ui.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
